From: Proton CRM <noreply@proton.local>
Subject: [PATCH] P10 task 4: the case-taxonomy admin page

Task 4's brief lists three files. Two of them landed in `56c3755`
(`features/taxonomy/router.py` and `taxonomy.manage` in `authz/seed.py`); the
third, `deploy/chatwoot-fork/patches/00NN-taxonomy-admin.patch`, did not. Four
endpoints were mounted and reachable, and no operator could reach them — the
shape this run has now shipped nine times and had to go back and fix nine times.
This is that file.

WHAT IT ADDS.
* `app/javascript/dashboard/api/protonTaxonomy.js` (new) — the four
  `/admin/taxonomy` calls, over `adminRequest` from `protonAdmin.js` so the
  devise_token_auth triplet `require_permission` needs is forwarded (patch 0030
  fixed exactly that for the earlier admin pages), plus one predicate for
  telling a "feature is off" 404 apart from a real failure.
* `app/javascript/dashboard/views/ProtonTaxonomyPage.vue` (new) — the tree, the
  node editor, the retire flow and the coverage report.
* `dashboard.routes.js` — `proton/taxonomy`, `meta.permissions:
  ['administrator']`.
* `Sidebar.vue` — a nav entry gated on `protonHasPermission('taxonomy.manage')`.

RETIRE, NEVER DELETE — AND THE PART THAT NEEDED DESIGNING. The store has no
delete method by construction: a category a historical case refers to has to stay
resolvable forever. But `retire_node` marks ONLY the node it is given and returns
that node's children that are still active, and `tree()` attaches a child to its
parent only when the parent is itself active. So retiring a parent leaves live
children that no longer appear in the tree, in any picker, or in the report — not
retired, not visible, not editable through any surface. A page that ignored that
return value would manufacture invisible taxonomy nodes on the operator's behalf.

So the page: warns BEFORE retiring, naming the number of active children and
saying plainly that they will not be retired with it; and AFTER retiring, renders
the returned list as a blocking panel with a Retire action per child, whose own
children (if any) join the queue. Dismissing it is possible and restates that the
nodes stay invisible, so leaving them is a decision rather than an oversight. This
is the brief's "returns a confirmation prompt, not an error", read as what the
backend actually does rather than as a modal.

TWO FLAGS, TWO DIFFERENT 404s. `/tree`, `/node` and `/node/:key/retire` 404 on
`TAXONOMY_ADMIN_ENABLED=false`; `/coverage` 404s on that OR
`CATEGORY_DEPARTMENT_MAPPING_ENABLED=false`. Each renders as its own "switched
off on this tenant, and here is the variable" message. Neither renders as an empty
table, because an empty table is a claim that the taxonomy is empty — a different
and worse thing to tell an operator than "you have not turned this on".

THE DEPARTMENT FIELD IS A MAPPING, NOT A ROUTE, AND THE UI SAYS SO. Nothing
applies a `dept_*` escalation label from it and nothing routes a case by it; the
coverage report is its only reader today. "Department" beside a category reads as
automatic routing, and this programme has already had to correct client material
that implied a mapping did more than it did.

PERMISSIONS. The nav gate, the route's `meta.permissions` and the backend's
`require_permission('taxonomy.manage')` agree, and only the last is a boundary.
Administrator-only matches `authz/seed.py`, which withholds the key from `agent`:
retiring a category changes what every report can group by. A caller without it
still gets the page read-only, with the reason named, rather than a blank screen.

DEPENDENCIES / STACKING. Stacks on **0057** for both modified files, and
transitively on everything 0057 stacks on — 0003/0025/0035/0041/0043/0053/0054 for
`Sidebar.vue` and `dashboard.routes.js`. Both new hunks insert directly after
0057's own added blocks (the alert-preferences nav entry and route), so a
line-number fixup to 0057 or anything below it cascades here. It also imports
`adminRequest` from `protonAdmin.js` and `useProtonPermissions` (0025, as amended
by 0031) but modifies neither, so it does not stack on their line numbers.

THE HUNKS WERE GENERATED BY `git diff` against a synthetic pre-image built by
replaying 0057 onto the transcribed reconstruction its own tests use, so the `@@`
arithmetic is git's rather than hand-written — the failure mode that broke 0056's
counts. The only hand edit afterwards was deleting the informational text git
appends after a hunk header's closing `@@`, which is derived from that
reconstruction's filler and would otherwise present filler as upstream structure;
`git apply --check` was re-run after that edit.

UNVERIFIED, STATED PLAINLY. Never applied to a real Chatwoot checkout and never
through a Cloud Build; this sandbox cannot clone upstream. What was actually run:
`git apply` (rc 0) onto the replayed pre-image, and `@vue/compiler-sfc` parse +
`compileScript` + `compileTemplate` of the resulting SFC, zero errors. Nothing
here has been seen rendering in a browser, no retire has been performed against a
real Firestore, and the two upstream-derived files' surrounding content is filler.
See `docs/analysis/2026-08-09-blocked-work-register.md`.
